# The behavior is the anchor and the arbiter

Take each unit as given; never re-derive intended behavior from the
implementation. When a test and the implementation disagree, the behavior
decides which is wrong: fix an implementation that diverges from a clause
(`FIX_IMPL`), fix a test that asserts what the behavior does not say, and
never make the implementation match your own judgment.

If a unit itself looks wrong (it contradicts another unit, describes a flow
that cannot exist, demands something clearly unintended, or makes no
observable claim at all), do NOT edit the corpus: record the problem and the
edit you would propose under that unit's `behavior_problems`, mark the
affected change `FAILED`, and move on.

