# Witnessing tests

A test declares the units it verifies with the `witnesses` marker, by
coordinate:

    @pytest.mark.witnesses("checkout.fresh-cart")
    def test_fresh_cart_checks_out() -> None: ...

    @pytest.mark.witnesses("checkout.single-use-coupons", partial="does not exercise concurrent redemptions")
    def test_spent_coupon_is_refused() -> None: ...

- `partial=` states precisely what the test does NOT cover; omit it when the
  test covers the unit fully; keep it exactly true on every test you touch.
- A test may carry several markers: mark every unit it genuinely witnesses,
  including an in-scope Rule verified in passing (note under which flow).
- The marker is registered repository-wide; one naming no unit of this corpus
  is a broken link, not a coverage gap, and fails the `links` gate.

# The objective: converge each unit's witnesses on its clauses

Make each unit's witnessing tests verify exactly its clauses plus its
in-scope invariant Rules, no more and no less, in both directions:

- Forward: every clause has a witnessing assertion that fails if the clause
  stops holding. Close a gap by building reach (missing setup is yours to
  build, not a reason to stop), never by widening assertions elsewhere.
- Backward: every assertion traces to a clause of the unit or an in-scope
  invariant; one that traces to neither is gold-plating: remove it (or the
  whole test, when all of it is redundant).

Before writing any assertion:

- Name the clause. State which clause the assertion witnesses, as written in
  the file and listed above, not scope you infer the author intended.
  If you cannot name one, do not write it.
- Match the clause's precision. Where a clause names a value, a status, or a
  target, assert that value; where it says only that something is refused or
  absent, assert that and nothing about how. Do not pin details the clause
  leaves open (an exact status the step does not name, page wording, header
  order).

An unwitnessed unit is not a special mode: expect to write new tests for it,
recorded under `CREATE_TEST`. A unit whose witnesses already verify exactly
its clauses needs no change. Before writing a new test, search the test roots
for one that already exercises the unit's surface, marked or not, and prefer
marking and extending it. Duplicating coverage that already exists is a
defect in this run.

# Verdicts

Report one verdict per unit, its end state after your work. Each clause is
closed-world or open-world:

- Closed-world: the claim ranges over a domain you can enumerate (a finite
  set of paths, statuses, states, origins). Every scenario and scenario
  outline is closed-world, and so are many Rules (a Rule naming four ways a
  token is invalid is four checks). Witness these completely.
- Open-world: the claim quantifies over an unbounded domain ("under any
  interleaving of requests", "over its whole lifetime"); no finite set of
  tests closes it. Witness it by attacking the property (present a spent code
  again, tamper with a token, request data without a session) and asserting
  the refusal, write a `partial=` note naming the exact quantifier no test
  can cross, and list the clause under `open_world_clauses`.

Witness a Rule's property itself, not just the flows scenarios describe. A
Rule's single clause is its name; one quantified over all states and
interleavings is open-world.

The verdicts:

- `FULL`: every clause has a witnessing assertion that fails if it stops
  holding; the trace names every clause and `open_world_clauses` is empty.
- `PARTIAL_STEADY`: at least one clause is open-world, named under
  `open_world_clauses`, and every closed-world clause is witnessed: the fixed
  point for such a unit; do not churn past it.
- `PARTIAL_IMPROVABLE`: at least one closed-world clause has no witnessing
  assertion; the next run re-targets it.
- `NONE`: no clause has a witnessing assertion. Legitimate only with a
  blocker or a behavior problem explaining why.

`FULL` is defined by the trace, not by the absence of `partial=` notes; never
clear a truthful note to claim it. Only an open-world quantifier earns
`PARTIAL_STEADY`: a note that names effort ("would need a fixture", "would
need a live backend") names your job now, not open-world residue.

